The fix is complete. Here's a summary of what I found and changed.

## Root cause

In Biome's HTML formatter, when two adjacent inline elements have no whitespace between them (e.g. `</span><span>`), the formatter uses a **token-borrowing** trick: the first element's closing `>` is *not* printed by its own `</...>` closing tag, but is instead handed to the next sibling to print at the start of its opening tag. This keeps the elements "touching" without inserting whitespace.

The bug: this borrowing was set up whenever the *next* sibling was any externally-whitespace-sensitive element — including self-closing **void** elements like `<br />` and `<img />`. But the code that actually prints the borrowed `>` before the next element (`format_element_with_borrowing` in `crates/biome_html_formatter/src/html/lists/element_list.rs`) only handled the `HtmlElement` case. For self-closing elements it fell into an `else` branch that just did `element.format()` and **silently dropped the borrowed `>` token**.

Since the first element's closing tag had already been told not to print its `>` (it was "borrowed"), that `>` was never emitted by anyone. The formatter's `printed_tokens` invariant checker then panicked with:

```
token has not been seen by the formatter: R_ANGLE ">" ... parent: HTML_CLOSING_ELEMENT
```

## The fix

In the `else` branch of `format_element_with_borrowing`, print the borrowed `>` (if any) before formatting the self-closing element:

```rust
} else {
    // For other element types (self-closing, etc.), the closing `>` borrowing
    // pattern doesn't apply because they have no separate closing tag. However,
    // we may still have borrowed a `>` from the previous sibling's closing tag
    // (e.g. `<code>x</code><br />`), which must be printed before this element so
    // the borrowed token is actually emitted and the elements stay touching.
    if let Some(ref borrowed_r_angle) = borrowed_sibling_r_angle {
        write!(f, [borrowed_r_angle.format()])?;
    }
    write!(f, [element.format()])
}
```

The reverse case (`<br /><code>`) is already safe because `closing_r_angle_token()` returns `None` for self-closing elements, so nothing gets borrowed from them.

## Verification

- Reproduced the original panic on `<code>x</code><br />`.
- After the fix, `<code>x</code><br />`, `<b>x</b><img …/>`, `<span>x</span><br>`, `<a>link</a><img src="a.png">`, `<p><code>x</code><br>y</p>`, and `<span>a</span><br><span>b</span>` all format without crashing, keeping the inline element and void element adjacent with no whitespace inserted. Already-normalized inputs are idempotent.
- Full `cargo test -p biome_html_formatter` suite passes (126 tests, no regressions).

Only the source file `crates/biome_html_formatter/src/html/lists/element_list.rs` was changed; no test files were touched.
